{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21ee7e39d99b57bc5adc", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Current Electricity - Test 4", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a89021a9"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902164", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a89020fd", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b566d"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6828"], "question": {"text": "The charge flowing in a conductor changes with time as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mfenced><mi>t</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>&#945;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#946;</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>&#947;</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>,&nbsp;where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#946;</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#947;</mi></math>&nbsp;are constants. Minimum value of current is:"}, "options": [{"id": "8944ab", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>&#946;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>&#947;</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a89020fe"}, {"id": "de447d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>&#947;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#946;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a89020ff"}, {"id": "b55f7a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>&#945;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#947;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902100"}, {"id": "bebab9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>&#946;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#947;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902101"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902102", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4750"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1e66f9ce3f243b4b15"], "question": {"text": "<p>A conducting wire of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math>, area of crosssection <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and electric resistivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math> is connected between the terminals of a battery. A potential difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> is developed between its ends, causing an electric current. If the length of the wire of the same material is doubled and the area of cross-section is halved, the resultant current would be___</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "e40452", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902103"}, {"id": "561162", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902104"}, {"id": "d4df95", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902105"}, {"id": "80fdb5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902106"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902107", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4761"], "concepts": ["60e567fb499d1e49588fa027"], "question": {"text": "Two resistors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>&nbsp; and &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>800</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> are connected in series across a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#8744;</mo></math> battery. The potential difference measured by a voltmeter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>k&#937;</mi></math> across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> resistor is close to:"}, "options": [{"id": "fb0553", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902108"}, {"id": "ae80ce", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902109"}, {"id": "6b97b5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890210a"}, {"id": "46485e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>95</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890210b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890210c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f36"], "concepts": ["6117b9d167dbe2603c3eacab"], "question": {"text": "Two identical cells each of emf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> are connected in parallel across a parallel combination of two resistors each of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>. A voltmeter connected in the circuit measures <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The internal resistance of each cell is :"}, "options": [{"id": "3742b2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890210d"}, {"id": "ea8326", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890210e"}, {"id": "829a7b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890210f"}, {"id": "a219dc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902110"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902111", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b64"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f04"], "question": {"text": "A motor operating on 100 V draws a current of 1 A. If the efficiency of the motor is $91.6 \\%$, then the loss of power in units of $\\mathrm{cal} / \\mathrm{s}$ is"}, "options": [{"id": "8a006c", "text": "$4$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902112"}, {"id": "535df9", "text": "$8.4$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902113"}, {"id": "c5d3ff", "text": "$2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902114"}, {"id": "5fd819", "text": "$6.2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902115"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902116", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e5"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52aa"], "question": {"text": "<p>The Wheatstone bridge shown in the figure below, gets balanced when the carbon resistor&nbsp;used as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> has the colour code (orange, red, brown). The resistors <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>, respectively. Assuming that the colour code for the carbon resistors gives their accurate values, the colour code for the carbon resistor, used as <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math>, would be</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fcffc70ba-29c5-41ff-997b-4d311ab1f26f-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 333px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "3b1d95", "text": "brown, blue, black.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902117"}, {"id": "075a25", "text": "brown, blue, brown.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902118"}, {"id": "97d75d", "text": "grey, black, brown.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902119"}, {"id": "24009d", "text": "red, green, brown.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890211a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890211b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4752"], "concepts": ["60ced5db19d2aa2b78a3a738"], "question": {"text": "A galvanometer having coil resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math> shows a full scale deflection for a current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mA</mi></math>. For it to measure a current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, the value of the shunt should be:"}, "options": [{"id": "b00bdc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890211c"}, {"id": "165b21", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890211d"}, {"id": "dcbdba", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>75</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890211e"}, {"id": "042f9a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>75</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890211f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902120", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60ced3320e34b73944ddfab7"], "concepts": ["60e567dc499d1e49588f9cb5"], "question": {"text": "<p>In the figure shown, the current in the&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>&nbsp;battery is close to :</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F0cab1338-8121-4a0a-8869-73f3cd83eeff-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 339px; height: 180px;\" />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "74ab8c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>71</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;from positive to negative terminal", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902121"}, {"id": "c4bc15", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;from positive to negative terminal&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902122"}, {"id": "c307cb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;from positive to negative terminal", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902123"}, {"id": "29103f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;from negative to postive terminal&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902124"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902125", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b566d"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdb66f9ce3f243b5643"], "question": {"text": "<p>A. The drift velocity of electrons decreases with the increase in the temperature of conductor.<br />B. The drift velocity is inversely proportional to the area of cross-section of given conductor.<br />C. The drift velocity does not depend on the applied potential difference to the conductor.<br />D. The drift velocity of electron is inversely proportional to the length of the conductor.<br />E. The drift velocity increases with the increase in the temperature of conductor.</p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "bfd983", "text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902126"}, {"id": "759bc4", "text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902127"}, {"id": "02f9c2", "text": "B and E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902128"}, {"id": "45d63c", "text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902129"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890212a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4761"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1e66f9ce3f243b4b19"], "question": {"text": "In the given circuit the internal resistance of the <math><mn>18</mn><mi>V</mi></math> cell is negligible. If <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>400&#160;</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100&#160;</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and the reading of an ideal voltmeter across <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></math> is <math><mn>5</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo></math> then the value of <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> will be:<br /><br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F1e5ca536-15b5-4697-b0e2-4047ca160acd-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 218px; height: 179px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "72484a", "text": "<math><mn>550</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890212b"}, {"id": "1c037c", "text": "<math><mn>300</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890212c"}, {"id": "7611c9", "text": "<math><mn>450</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890212d"}, {"id": "c8b037", "text": "<math><mn>230</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890212e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890212f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b64"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b25"], "question": {"text": "A resistor develops <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>500</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math> of thermal energy in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> when a current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is passed through it. If the current is increased from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, what will be the energy developed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "4f3ea9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1500</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902130"}, {"id": "2cab0d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902131"}, {"id": "b6b446", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>500</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902132"}, {"id": "043128", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902133"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902134", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4752"], "concepts": ["6162fb276ed13040ec84ba05"], "question": {"text": "<p>For designing a voltmeter of range <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> and an ammeter of range <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mA</mi></math> using a galvanometer which has a coil of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>54</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8486;</mo></math> showing a full scale deflection for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mA</mi></math> as in figure.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fad9d9daf-7ae9-438a-81a9-9d8b755cbfee.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 224px;\" /></p><p>(A) for voltmeter&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mo>&#8486;</mo></math></p><p>(B) for ammeter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8486;</mo></math></p><p>(C) for ammeter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8486;</mo></math></p><p>(D)&nbsp;for voltmeter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mo>&#8486;</mo></math></p><p>(E)&nbsp;for voltmeter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>500</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8486;</mo></math></p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "5c9153", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902135"}, {"id": "18f81a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902136"}, {"id": "44fff6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902137"}, {"id": "521c6a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902138"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902139", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f36"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e858a"], "question": {"text": "In the given circuit, an ideal voltmeter connected across the <math><mn>10</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> resistance reads <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> . The internal resistance <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math> , of each cell is:<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fefba144b-7e97-472d-8aaa-8b6ac27b0500-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 249px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "e3915f", "text": "<math><mn>0</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890213a"}, {"id": "56b768", "text": "<math><mn>1.5</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890213b"}, {"id": "6300b2", "text": "<math><mn>0.5</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890213c"}, {"id": "7cfcb8", "text": "<math><mn>1</mn><mi> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890213d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890213e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60ced3320e34b73944ddfab7"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdb66f9ce3f243b5640"], "question": {"text": "In the circuit shown, the potential difference between $\\mathrm{A}$ and $B$ is\n\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FhV08eiGJ8CdHXXYggRMCLoJpA9SmTNg9bcAnQOKcGVo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">"}, "options": [{"id": "89e3ea", "text": "$1 \\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890213f"}, {"id": "cf1b82", "text": "$2 \\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902140"}, {"id": "871893", "text": "$3 V$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902141"}, {"id": "48746a", "text": "$6 \\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902142"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902143", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e5"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8edb"], "question": {"text": "The resistance of the meter bridge AB in given figure is $4 \\Omega$. With a cell of emf $\\varepsilon=0.5 \\mathrm{~V}$ and rheostat resistance $\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{h}}=2 \\Omega$ the null point is obtained at some point J. When the cell is replaced by another one of emf $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_{2}$ the same null point $\\mathrm{J}$ is found for $\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{h}}=6 \\Omega$. The $\\operatorname{emf} \\varepsilon_{2}$ is:\n\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F10XxhZc_WgumdrzdI9sRk7osdQr1aehCflZqE-eFEjM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">"}, "options": [{"id": "409051", "text": "$0.4 \\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902144"}, {"id": "6d1302", "text": "$0.3 \\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902145"}, {"id": "e47f29", "text": "$0.6 \\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902146"}, {"id": "7ef56a", "text": "$0.5 \\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902147"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902148", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4750"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1e66f9ce3f243b4b15"], "question": {"text": "A wire of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math> is cut into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math> equal parts. If these parts are joined parallely, then resultant resistance will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "6b43e8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>25</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902149"}, {"id": "d83309", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890214a"}, {"id": "392494", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mi>R</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890214b"}, {"id": "846e03", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mi>R</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890214c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890214d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4752"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4702"], "question": {"text": "<p>Three voltmeters, all having different internal resistances are joined as shown in figure. When some potential difference is applied across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, their readings are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>. Choose the correct option.</p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fc5aa5b4d-e4a0-4f42-8941-51ccc2212d77.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 146px;\"/>"}, "options": [{"id": "fa505c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890214e"}, {"id": "4e2928", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890214f"}, {"id": "249e42", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902150"}, {"id": "446945", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902151"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902152", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b566d"], "concepts": ["6162fb236ed13040ec84b739"], "question": {"text": "Mobility of electrons in a semiconductor is defined as the ratio of their drift velocity to the applied electric field. If, for an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>N</mtext></math>-type semiconductor, the density of electrons is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>19</mn></msup><mi>&#160;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> and their mobility is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1.6</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then the resistivity of the semiconductor (since it is an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>N</mtext></math>-type semiconductor contribution of holes is ignored) is close to:"}, "options": [{"id": "45a8c4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0.2</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902153"}, {"id": "4febc2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902154"}, {"id": "122fdb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902155"}, {"id": "755ccf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0.4</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902156"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902157", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4761"], "concepts": ["60e567fb499d1e49588fa027"], "question": {"text": "A wire of resistance R is bent into an equilateral triangle and an identical wire is bent into $a$ square. The ratio of resistance between the two end points of an edge of the triangle to that of the square is\nOptions"}, "options": [{"id": "d5cf5c", "text": "8/9", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902158"}, {"id": "0ef954", "text": "$27 / 32$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902159"}, {"id": "21ce3a", "text": "$32 / 27$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890215a"}, {"id": "3e41be", "text": "$9 / 8$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890215b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890215c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f36"], "concepts": ["6117b9d167dbe2603c3eacab"], "question": {"text": "<p>Two cells of emf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> with internal resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively are connected in series to an external resistor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> (see figure). The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;at which the potential difference across the terminals of the first cell becomes zero is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fef8bd153-5129-4b78-a69d-fa68ea87322f-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 240px; height: 167px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "e4cc52", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890215d"}, {"id": "608535", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890215e"}, {"id": "4040eb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890215f"}, {"id": "a950bf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902160"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:24.290Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:24.290Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a89021a7"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a89021a5", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902189", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b566d"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa021"], "question": {"text": "<p>For the network shown below, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;_____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fe2562687-5019-48f0-9587-a4635a7abff5-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 130px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "1b8cc5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890218a"}, {"id": "4e0054", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890218b"}, {"id": "b0cfd7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890218c"}, {"id": "d864af", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890218d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890218e", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4750"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1e66f9ce3f243b4b15"], "question": {"text": "A wire of resistance $R$ and radius $r$ is stretched till its radius became $r / 2$. If new resistance of the stretched wire is $x R$, then value of $x$ is _______"}, "options": [{"id": "fa6243", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890218f"}, {"id": "0a5513", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902190"}, {"id": "8875d5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902191"}, {"id": "4a56e2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902192"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902193", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4761"], "concepts": ["60e567fb499d1e49588fa027"], "question": {"text": "<p>A current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> enters one corner <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> of an equilateral triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;having <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> wires of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> each and leaves by the corner <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;The currents <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> in ampere is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F183f227d-b2fe-4945-bcf0-d68c5fda31fa-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 181px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "89e2af", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902194"}, {"id": "f05942", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902195"}, {"id": "fd3930", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902196"}, {"id": "7594fb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902197"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902198", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f36"], "concepts": ["6117b9d167dbe2603c3eacab"], "question": {"text": "Two identical cells each of emf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> are connected in series across a&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> resistance. An ideal voltmeter connected across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> resistance reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The internal resistance of each cell is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "572968", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a8902199"}, {"id": "0db4b1", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890219a"}, {"id": "ca9f11", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890219b"}, {"id": "5dc8ff", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890219c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890219d", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfade"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e5"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8edb"], "question": {"text": "In a meter bridge experiment <math><mi>S</mi></math> is a standard resistance. <math><mi>R</mi></math> is a resistance wire. It is found that balancing length is <math><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>.</mo></math> If <math><mi>R</mi></math> is replaced by a wire of hall length and half diameter that of <math><mi>R</mi></math> of same material, then the balancing distance <math><mi>l</mi><mi>&#39;</mi></math> (in <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> ) will now be ______________.<br /><br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F3a084e1c-0d66-4a24-893e-c9220c259f09-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 186px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "002922", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890219e"}, {"id": "ce7365", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a890219f"}, {"id": "d931df", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f08c677dc36a89021a0"}, {"id": "086b69", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de